1. **Gold data remains accessible through `run_command`.** [run_agent_eval.py:290](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma-wt/codex-eval-runner/eval/run_agent_eval.py:290) selects the subprocess backend, whose commands execute on the host without filesystem isolation. Clearing `task` and disabling secret hydration protects the object boundary, but the agent can still read the host inventory containing `patch`, `test_patch`, and `FAIL_TO_PASS`. Self-check outputs also serialize gold patches at [line 387](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma-wt/codex-eval-runner/eval/run_agent_eval.py:387). I confirmed unrestricted access using a harmless `AGENTS.md` outside the synthetic workspace: exit 0, read succeeded. An enforced filesystem boundary must hide inventory and verification artifacts from agent commands.

2. **The supplied context loses the resolved graph and embedding paths.** [run_agent_eval.py:336](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma-wt/codex-eval-runner/eval/run_agent_eval.py:336) constructs `SwegemmaContext` without `graph_dir=config.graph_dir` or `embeddings_dir=config.embeddings_dir`. `EvalConfig` can resolve these under `--data-dir`, but the context retains relative `data/graphs` and `data/embeddings`. Because the harness reuses this context, graph tools search the wrong location. This can systematically depress quality scores and invalidate comparisons involving graph tools.

3. **`tool_seconds` does not measure individual tool execution.** [run_agent_eval.py:201](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma-wt/codex-eval-runner/eval/run_agent_eval.py:201) subtracts trace event timestamps. Installed ADK gathers batch results and merges their responses; `SessionTrace` timestamps those responses when the merged event is recorded. A 0.1-second read batched with a 10-second command can therefore receive approximately 10 seconds too. Summed tool time can exceed agent wall time and distort test-time share. FIFO additionally cannot identify overlapping same-name calls: reordered responses corrupt individual durations and `max`, although a fully matched batch’s aggregate subtraction can remain unchanged. Instrument execution boundaries with call IDs and monotonic timestamps; trace pairing alone cannot repair merged timestamps.

4. **Resume silently mixes runner implementations.** [run_agent_eval.py:109](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma-wt/codex-eval-runner/eval/run_agent_eval.py:109) excludes `git_sha` from header identity despite recording it. I reproduced acceptance of a changed SHA. After a runner or dependency-emulation fix, remaining tasks can use new behavior while completed rows—and the header’s provenance—remain old. Inventory contents and harness/environment versions are also unpinned. Refuse incompatible resumes using an evaluation-protocol identity and input/environment fingerprints.

5. **Runner and gold calibration now use different package environments.** [run_agent_eval.py:62](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma-wt/codex-eval-runner/eval/run_agent_eval.py:62) removes `_host_env.pth`; standalone [gold_calibration.py:178](/home/ubuntu/work/gemma-wt/codex-eval-runner/eval/kaggle/gold_calibration.py:178) does not apply that isolation. Removing harness packages is consistent with the intended Docker-image emulation; I would keep that correction. However, the existing gold clean list was calibrated with a different import environment. Share isolation setup between both entrypoints and rerun calibration/control checks before relying on exclusions. Two startup passers cannot validate the entire clean list.

